NXR-161|Profile RAM reconciliation|Resource qualification|The release gate shall reconcile every allocated RAM section and boot-reserved stack against the declared board memory map.|Current static allocation contracts do not establish a complete per-profile board reconciliation.|Sum all disjoint load and runtime ranges; reject an omitted boot stack and any overlap or address overflow.|Compare linker map and ELF program headers with a separately authored board manifest; inject each omission.|NXR-081,NXR-025|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Zero unexplained RAM bytes; board capacity is a hard ceiling.|include/rtos.hpp,src/kernel.cpp,src/scheduler.hpp,docs/memory.rst|Current static allocation contracts do not establish a complete per-profile board reconciliation.
NXR-162|Allocation gate detection controls|Resource qualification|The allocator audit shall prove detection of each forbidden allocator family using deliberately linked negative fixtures.|A passing symbol scan alone cannot show that archive extraction and renamed wrappers are detected.|Fixtures referencing malloc, new, aligned allocation and allocator wrappers fail; a heap-free control passes.|Build positive and negative ELF fixtures with the same release flags and linker garbage collection.|NXR-081,NXR-025|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Zero permitted allocator references; fixtures remain host build artifacts.|include/rtos.hpp,src/kernel.cpp,src/scheduler.hpp,docs/memory.rst|A passing symbol scan alone cannot show that archive extraction and renamed wrappers are detected.
NXR-163|Critical section measurement coverage|Resource qualification|The qualification report shall enumerate every interrupt-masked path and its input-dependent work bound.|The kernel uses local interrupt serialization, so latency claims need coverage of all masked work.|Queue maximum copies, waiter scans, donation chains and trace operations each have a source path and measured bound.|Instrument target mask entry and exit; test minimum and maximum configured capacities and calibrated probe overhead.|NXR-081,NXR-025|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Per-profile maximum mask duration is declared before measurement; no unbounded callback under mask.|include/rtos.hpp,src/kernel.cpp,src/scheduler.hpp,docs/memory.rst|The kernel uses local interrupt serialization, so latency claims need coverage of all masked work.
NXR-164|Stack evidence completeness|Resource qualification|The stack report shall distinguish untouched sentinel bytes from proven unused call-chain space.|Watermarks are observations and cannot independently bound unexecuted paths.|A deliberately deep call and an interrupt at its deepest point are detected; incomplete scanning is reported as incomplete.|Combine compiler stack usage, call-chain review and target watermark fixtures including boot and fault paths.|NXR-081,NXR-025|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Every enabled stack has a byte ceiling including nested exception frames.|include/rtos.hpp,src/kernel.cpp,src/scheduler.hpp,docs/memory.rst|Watermarks are observations and cannot independently bound unexecuted paths.
NXR-165|Feature removal differential|Resource qualification|The release gate shall compare disabled-feature firmware against enabled firmware using identical toolchains and application behavior.|Optional source compilation does not guarantee that associated static storage disappears.|Disabled DMA, logging and tracing remove their symbols and reserved pools; a hidden residual pool fails.|Retain map diffs and ELF symbol checks for one toggle at a time.|NXR-081,NXR-025|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Disabled feature contributes zero feature-owned RAM and code beyond documented shared dependencies.|include/rtos.hpp,src/kernel.cpp,src/scheduler.hpp,docs/memory.rst|Optional source compilation does not guarantee that associated static storage disappears.
NXR-166|Timing units provenance|Resource qualification|Every timing result shall carry target frequency, counter width, conversion rule and measurement origin.|Host elapsed time and simulated timer advancement were previously easy to confuse.|Changing frequency without regenerating the profile rejects qualification; host seconds cannot populate target-time fields.|Exercise two clock profiles and counter wrap with known interval fixtures.|NXR-081,NXR-025|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Conversions use checked arithmetic and a declared rounding bound of at most one target tick.|include/rtos.hpp,src/kernel.cpp,src/scheduler.hpp,docs/memory.rst|Host elapsed time and simulated timer advancement were previously easy to confuse.
NXR-167|Capacity sweep qualification|Resource qualification|The qualification gate shall exercise legal minimum, representative and maximum resource capacities.|Testing one default capacity misses index and fairness boundary defects.|Thread, queue and waiter boundaries reject excess operations without mutation at every selected capacity.|Run independent reference models and target boundary fixtures; retain configuration identity per case.|NXR-081,NXR-025|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|No capacity-dependent allocation; total execution budget declared for each sweep.|include/rtos.hpp,src/kernel.cpp,src/scheduler.hpp,docs/memory.rst|Testing one default capacity misses index and fairness boundary defects.
NXR-168|Idle residency accounting|Resource qualification|CPU residency reports shall identify measured busy and idle intervals without counting blocked host simulation time as target execution.|Existing metrics need a calibrated interpretation before they support performance claims.|A controlled idle-heavy workload and busy loop produce ordered residency results; totals reconcile within declared sampling error.|Compare target counter accounting with external GPIO intervals and explicit sampling overhead.|NXR-081,NXR-025|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Accounting work is constant per transition; error ceiling declared in target counter units.|include/rtos.hpp,src/kernel.cpp,src/scheduler.hpp,docs/memory.rst|Existing metrics need a calibrated interpretation before they support performance claims.
NXR-169|Reproducible optimized builds|Resource qualification|Qualified firmware and interpreter builds shall record effective compiler options and require the designated optimized profile to use -O3.|Requested optimization must be verified from effective commands rather than preset names.|A substituted -O2 interpreter or wrong language standard rejects the optimized qualification label.|Inspect compilation databases and embedded build identity; execute a wrong-flags negative fixture.|NXR-081,NXR-025|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|C++20 interpreter; -O3 release profile; debug and sanitizer profiles remain separately named.|include/rtos.hpp,src/kernel.cpp,src/scheduler.hpp,docs/memory.rst|Requested optimization must be verified from effective commands rather than preset names.
NXR-170|Performance regression envelope|Resource qualification|The performance gate shall compare fixed workloads only under matching hardware, compiler, features and measurement methods.|An observed speed difference is meaningless when build or measurement conditions change.|Changed profile identities prevent direct speedup claims; a seeded slow-path injection crosses and fails the recorded threshold.|Retain baseline distributions, repetitions, machine identity and confidence method alongside raw observations.|NXR-081,NXR-025|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Per-workload threshold and repeat count fixed before candidate execution.|include/rtos.hpp,src/kernel.cpp,src/scheduler.hpp,docs/memory.rst|An observed speed difference is meaningless when build or measurement conditions change.
